# Line endings for scripts, pinned so a checkout or a ZIP download gets the ones the
# interpreter needs, whatever the user's core.autocrlf says. Everything is stored with LF
# in the repository; only the working-tree copy differs.

# Windows scripts: CRLF. cmd.exe mis-parses labels and multi-line blocks in an LF-only
# batch file, and Windows editors expect CRLF in PowerShell files. A UTF-8 byte-order mark
# is content, not a line ending, so the BOM some .ps1 files carry is left alone.
*.cmd   text eol=crlf
*.bat   text eol=crlf
*.ps1   text eol=crlf
*.psm1  text eol=crlf
*.psd1  text eol=crlf

# POSIX shell scripts: LF, including on Windows, where core.autocrlf=true would otherwise
# check them out with CRLF and Git Bash or WSL would fail on the stray carriage returns.
*.sh    text eol=lf
